-- title:
--   Lemma 3.2 (corrected: open-loop adjoint system with cross terms through the measure argument) — $N$-player stochastic maximum principle
-- statement:
--   Suppose that Assumption 3.1 holds, and let $\boldsymbol\alpha^N=(\alpha^{N,1},\dots,\alpha^{N,N})$ be a Nash equilibrium of the $N$-player game, with state process $\mathbf X^N$. Then there is a solution $(\mathbf X^N,(Y^{i,j}),(Z^{i,j,l}))$ of the open-loop adjoint system (3.2′) such that for each $i=1,\dots,N$,
--   $$\alpha^{N,i}_t=\hat\alpha\big(X^{N,i}_t,Y^{i,i}_t,Z^{i,i,i}_t,Z^{i,i,0}_t,m^N_{\mathbf X^N_t}\big),\qquad dt\otimes d\mathbb P\text{-a.e.}$$
--
--   This is the necessary condition that turns an open-loop Nash equilibrium into a system of forward–backward SDEs. When $b,\sigma,\sigma^0$ do not depend on the measure (always the case under Assumptions 2.4–2.5 and 2.11–2.12), (3.2′) for $Y^{N,i}:=Y^{i,i}$ is the printed system (3.2).
--
--   **Formalization Note** The versions of the Lions derivatives in (3.2′) are assumed to be Lions derivatives of the data (`AreLions`), which the three cases of Assumption 3.1 allow.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - correction: (3.2), whose backward equation for $Y^{N,i}$ carries only $D_xH+\frac1ND_mH(X^{N,i},\dots,m^N,X^{N,i})$ → the open-loop adjoint system (3.2′) with adjoints $Y^{i,j}$ and the cross terms $\frac1N\sum_k[D_mb(X^k,\alpha^k,m,X^j)^\top Y^{i,k}+D_m\sigma(\dots)^\top Z^{i,k,k}+D_m\sigma^0(\dots)^\top Z^{i,k,0}]$; reason: the printed lemma is false when $b$, $\sigma$ or $\sigma^0$ depends on $m$. Counterexample: $n=k=d=1$, $T=1$, $m_0=\delta_1$, $\sigma=\sigma^0=0$, $b(x,a,m)=a+\frac12\int y\,m(dy)$, $L=a^2/2+x^2/2$, $G=x^2/2$ satisfy Assumptions 2.17 and 2.19 ($C_H=3/4$, $C_G=1$) and 2.20; in the $N=2$ game, (3.2) together with $\alpha^{N,i}=-Y^{N,i}$ forces $X^{N,1}\equiv0$, contradicting $X^{N,1}_0=1$, while the equilibrium satisfies (3.2′)
--   - encoding: the Lions derivatives $D_mb,D_m\sigma,D_m\sigma^0,D_mL,D_mG$ in (3.2′) → versions `V` with the hypothesis `AreLions D V` that they are Lions derivatives of the data; reason: fixes the meaning of the symbols; satisfiable under each case of Assumption 3.1, and the system evaluates them only at atoms of the empirical measure
--   - reading: "$\alpha^i_t$" and "$D_mH(\dots,m^N_{\mathbf X^N_t})$" without a point argument → $\alpha^{N,i}_t$ and the point argument of the player; reason: typographical; compare (3.4), (3.5)
--   - encoding: $D_xH$ in the adjoint equation → $D_x[b\cdot y+\sigma\cdot z+\sigma^0\cdot z^0](X^j,\alpha^{N,j},m)+\delta_{ij}D_xL$ evaluated at the equilibrium controls; reason: equal to $D_xH$ at the minimizer wherever $H$ is differentiable; the form of (3.4), (3.5)
--   - generalization: the canonical spaces $\Omega^N=(\mathbb R^n)^N\times\mathcal C^{N+1}$ with product measure, embedded in one another (pp.9–10) → one probability space carrying $\xi^1,\xi^2,\dots$ (law $m_0$), $W^0$ and $W^1,W^2,\dots$, mutually independent, Brownian paths continuous (`Setup`); reason: the canonical product space is an instance, so the statement specializes to the paper's
--   - generalization: $d,k,n\in\mathbb N=\{1,2,\dots\}$ → any $n,k,d\ge 0$; reason: the source's dimensions are instances
--   - encoding: augmented filtrations $\mathbb F^N$, $\mathbb F^0$ → the raw filtrations generated by $\xi^1,\dots,\xi^N,W^0,\dots,W^N$ and by $W^0$; reason: every process progressive for the augmented filtration has a raw-progressive version ([41, Prop. 1.2.1], cited p.10); controls and states enter only up to versions
--   - encoding: continuous processes → processes with almost surely continuous paths on $[0,T]$; reason: on a general probability space continuity holds outside a null set; suprema and integrals do not see null sets
--   - encoding: players $i\in\{1,\dots,N\}$ → players `i : Fin N`, player $i$ of the paper is index $i-1$; reason: relabelling
--   - encoding: $\mathbb R^{n\times d}$ with the Frobenius norm and $A\cdot B=\mathrm{tr}(A^\top B)$ → `EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n × Fin d)`; reason: same norm and inner product
--   - encoding: a Nash equilibrium $\boldsymbol\alpha^N$ and "the state process corresponding to $\boldsymbol\alpha^N$" → `IsNashN`: admissible $\boldsymbol\alpha^N$, its state process $\mathbf X^N$ solving (2.3), compared against every admissible deviation and every state process of the deviation; reason: strong solutions are unique under Lipschitz data, so "every" and "the" agree
--   - encoding: $J^{N,i}$, $J_m$ real valued (footnote 1, p.12) → the costs of the equilibrium are finite, and only deviations with finite (integrable) cost are compared; reason: under the paper's assumptions every cost is finite, so the compared sets coincide
--   - encoding: "Lipschitz in all arguments", "bounded", "$C^1$" for maps on $\mathbb R^p\times\mathcal P_2(\mathbb R^n)$ → `LipXM`, `BddXM`, `IsC1` with $\mathcal W_2$ in the measure and the max-norm on the product of the other arguments; reason: equivalent norms give the same notions up to the (existential) constants
--   - encoding: $\hat\alpha$, the unique minimizer of (3.1) → `alphaHat`: a minimizer chosen by `Classical.choose`, `0` if none exists; reason: the minimizer exists and is unique under Assumptions 2.5, 2.12 and 2.19 (p.19)
--   - encoding: backward equations with terminal condition → forward Itô form from the random initial value together with the terminal condition (`IsItoProc` + a.s. terminal value); reason: the integral form of a BSDE
-- source:
--   Jackson & Tangpi, Quantitative Convergence for Displacement Monotone Mean Field Games with Controlled Volatility, arXiv:2304.04543v2, Lemma 3.2 and (3.2), (3.3), pp.19–20; Remarks 3.3–3.4 p.20

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_CtrlVolMFG_IsSol_3_6

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory
open scoped ENNReal NNReal

namespace CtrlVolMFG

/-- Lemma 3.2 (Jackson & Tangpi, arXiv:2304.04543v2, pp.19–20), corrected: suppose that
Assumption 3.1 holds and let `αᴺ = (α^{N,1}, …, α^{N,N})` be a Nash equilibrium of the `N`-player
game with state process `Xᴺ`. Then for each `i`,
`α^{N,i}_t = α̂(X^{N,i}_t, Y^{i,i}_t, Z^{i,i,i}_t, Z^{i,i,0}_t, m^N_{X_t})`, `dt × dℙ`-a.e.,
for some solution `(Xᴺ, Y, Z)` of the open-loop adjoint system (3.2′) (`IsSol_3_2c`).

**Formalization Note** (Correction.) The printed system (3.2) keeps only player `i`'s own adjoint
`Y^{N,i}` and the own-player term `(1/N) D_mH`. When `b`, `σ` or `σ⁰` depends on `m`, player `i`'s
state enters the other players' dynamics through `m^N_X`, and the adjoints `Y^{i,j}`, `j ≠ i`,
with the cross terms `(1/N) ∑_{k} D_mb(Xᵏ, αᵏ, m, Xⁱ)ᵀY^{i,k} + …`, are needed. Counterexample to
the printed lemma: `n = k = d = 1`, `T = 1`, `m₀ = δ₁`, `σ = σ⁰ = 0`, `b(x, a, m) = a + ∫y m(dy)/2`,
`L = a²/2 + x²/2`, `G = x²/2` satisfy Assumptions 2.17, 2.19 (with `C_H = 3/4`, `C_G = 1`), and the
`N = 2` Nash equilibrium does not satisfy (3.2). When `b, σ, σ⁰` do not depend on `m` (always
under Assumptions 2.4–2.5 and 2.11–2.12) the equations for `Y^{i,i}` are (3.2). The hypothesis
`AreLions D V` says that the versions `V` used in (3.2′) are Lions derivatives of the data. -/
theorem lemma_3_2 {n k d : ℕ} {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (S : Setup n d Ω)
    (D : Data n k d) (V : Derivs n k d) (T : ℝ≥0) (hT : 0 < T) (h31 : Assumption_3_1 D V T)
    (hV : AreLions D V) (N : ℕ) (hN : 0 < N) (α : Fin N → ℝ≥0 → Ω → Rn k)
    (X : Fin N → ℝ≥0 → Ω → Rn n) (hα : IsNashN S D T N α X) :
    ∃ (Y : Fin N → Fin N → ℝ≥0 → Ω → Rn n)
      (Z : Fin N → Fin N → Option (Fin N) → ℝ≥0 → Ω → Mat n d),
      IsSol_3_2c S D V T N α X Y Z ∧
      ∀ i : Fin N, EqDtDP S.P T (α i) (fun t ω => D.alphaHat (X i t ω) (Y i i t ω)
        (Z i i (some i) t ω) (Z i i none t ω) (empP2 fun l => X l t ω)) := by sorry

end CtrlVolMFG
